# Normalize line endings: text files are stored LF in the repo, regardless
# of any contributor's core.autocrlf setting. This is the shared, in-repo
# source of truth — autocrlf is per-machine and unreliable.
* text=auto

# Visual Studio tooling expects CRLF in the working tree for these.
# (Still normalized to LF in the index; checked out as CRLF.)
*.sln             text eol=crlf
*.vcxproj         text eol=crlf
*.vcxproj.filters text eol=crlf

# Shell scripts must be LF in the WORKING TREE too, not just the index. With
# core.autocrlf=true (the default on Windows) they otherwise check out as CRLF,
# and bash chokes on the trailing \r: `set -euo pipefail` becomes `pipefail\r`
# and the script dies on line 6. That is not hypothetical — Oracle/jpegenc/
# build.sh was checked out CRLF and unrunnable from Git Bash. These scripts now
# build oracles in CI as well, so they have to work on both platforms.
*.sh              text eol=lf

# Committed binary test fixtures. `* text=auto` classifies these correctly today,
# but that is a content heuristic and a small codestream could plausibly trip it —
# a single CRLF rewrite silently corrupts a fixture into an undecodable stream,
# which surfaces as a baffling codec bug rather than as a diff. Be explicit.
*.jb2 binary

# The JPEG 2000 fixtures, and this is not a precaution — it already happened.
# Git classifies by looking for NUL bytes, and a PGM raster need not contain one:
# `flat64.pgm` is 4096 bytes of 0x80, and `odd1x1.pgm` is a single pixel, so both
# were staged as TEXT and would have been CRLF-rewritten on a Windows checkout.
# Any 0x0A in a raster becomes 0x0D 0x0A and the fixture stops being the image it
# was generated from -- while the .j2k beside it, which does contain NULs, would
# have been left alone. The test comparing them would then fail in a way that
# looks like a decoder bug.
*.j2k binary
*.jp2 binary
*.pgm binary
*.ppm binary
*.raw binary

# Vendored C headers (the stb_image_write.h the jpegenc oracle downloads at build
# time) — mark them so GitHub language stats don't attribute them to this repo.
*.h linguist-vendored

# WebP fixtures and benchmark inputs: RIFF binaries, never to be text-normalized.
*.webp binary
